WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.935 --> 00:00:04.037
EDWARD BUCKLES, JR:
<i> We woke up, and my mama
 was all over the place,</i>

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.905
like, "Yo, we leavin'.
Like, yo,

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:07.607
I'm not asking you.
I'm telling you."

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:13.413
<i> Since the storm, it seems like
 everybody just moved on.</i>

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:16.583
<i> In America, especially
 during disasters,</i>

00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:18.851
<i> Black children
 are not even a thought.</i>

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:20.787
Have you ever talked
about this before?

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:22.955
No, I haven't.

00:00:23.222 --> 00:00:24.290
BUCKLES: Why, you think?

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:27.760
I don't know. Nobody
really there to ask me.

00:00:29.228 --> 00:00:32.565
BUCKLES:
<i> Hurricane Katrina caused
one of the biggest dispersements</i>

00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:34.534
<i> of Black people in history.</i>

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:37.904
<i> After losing so much, why
 wouldn't anyone ask</i>

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.139
<i> if we were okay?</i>

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.976
<i> Nobody ever asked the children
 how they were doin',</i>

00:00:43.876 --> 00:00:44.944
<i> so I am.</i>

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:47.147
Katrina Babies,<i> take one.</i>

00:00:53.385 --> 00:00:54.754
Friday, I was at school,

00:00:54.787 --> 00:00:56.422
walking the hallways
with my friends.

00:00:56.789 --> 00:00:58.625
<i> By Monday, I was on top
 a fucking roof.</i>

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:02.495
SPEAKER:<i> Everything you consider
 as part of who you are</i>

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:05.297
<i> was reduced to a trash bag.</i>

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:07.066
SPEAKER 2:
<i> Am I gonna die? I don't know.</i>

00:01:07.099 --> 00:01:09.268
I just was like,
I'm not supposed to be here.

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:12.005
BUCKLES:<i> The New Orleans
 that we knew was gone.</i>

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.944
<i> When I got back after Katrina,
 I naively thought</i>

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:19.679
<i> that everything
 was gon' be normal again.</i>

00:01:20.579 --> 00:01:23.316
SPEAKER 3:<i> The feel of community
in New Orleans is different now.</i>

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:26.119
SPEAKER:<i> Your neighborhood
 was crucial to who you were.</i>

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:29.022
What we knew to be true
is gone.

00:01:31.156 --> 00:01:34.093
<i> Think about what that does
 to a community.</i>

00:01:34.126 --> 00:01:35.195
<i> It destabilizes it.</i>

00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:41.267
SPEAKER 4:<i> I feel like nobody
 should be that young</i>

00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:42.601
<i> going through shit like that.</i>

00:01:43.035 --> 00:01:45.138
SPEAKER 5:<i> They're in homes,
 and they don't feel safe.</i>

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:46.639
<i> What does that do
 to our children?</i>

00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:49.109
<i> It wreaks havoc
 on their well-being.</i>

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:56.615
BUCKLES:
<i>Katrina is becoming a folktale,
 and we're the storytellers.</i>

00:01:57.516 --> 00:01:59.652
<i> Resilience
 is when you still find a way</i>

00:01:59.685 --> 00:02:01.988
<i> to have your head up
 and help yourself.</i>

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:06.359
<i> Sometimes, I feel like
 resilience is used as a tool</i>

00:02:06.959 --> 00:02:09.228
<i> because they want people
 to think,</i>

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:11.330
<i> "Oh, no, everything is okay.
 These people are good.</i>

00:02:11.363 --> 00:02:14.066
<i>They're strong. Look at how much
 they've overcome."</i>

00:02:14.633 --> 00:02:16.802
<i> It's for me to say
 we're not resilient.</i>

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:18.737
<i> It's for me to say
 what is resilient.</i>

00:02:18.804 --> 00:02:19.873
<i> It's not for you.</i>

00:02:21.206 --> 00:02:23.676
SPEAKER:<i> Being able to tell
 my Katrina story</i>

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:26.412
<i> has been part
 of my healing process.</i>

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:29.682
BUCKLES:
<i> Even though we are resilient,</i>

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:31.584
<i> we can never get back
 what was lost.</i>

